course: dictionary
lesson: 11017
title: Words for places by the water
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Waterside can be a noun or an adjective. As a noun, the waterside is the land along the edge of a river, lake or sea. «Water voles eat grasses, reeds and other waterside plants, not fish.» The plants grow by the water, so waterside tells you where they are.
explain: Check the clues around the word. The voles dig burrows in the bank, and the bank is the sloping land at the water's edge. So waterside must mean the land beside the water. Try your meaning in the sentence: «other plants that grow by the water» still makes sense.
explain: As an adjective, waterside describes something found by the water, such as a waterside cafe. When you see waterside before a thing, ask yourself: is this thing near the water?
fact: Waterside means the land along the edge of a river, lake or sea.
fact: As an adjective, waterside describes things found by the water, like «a waterside cafe» or «waterside plants».
fact: Reeds and willows are waterside plants, and they like wet soil.
fact: Water voles dig burrows in the bank, and some of the entrances are under the water.
fact: Children are told to be careful at the waterside, because the banks can be slippery.
exercise: «Water voles eat grasses, reeds and other waterside plants, not fish.» What does waterside mean here? | *Growing by the water | Hidden under the ground | Far away in the desert | Made of glass
exercise: In safety lessons, children are told to be careful at the waterside. Why? | Because the waterside is very dry | Because the water is always warm | Because there are no plants there | *Because the banks can be slippery
exercise: «We sat in a waterside cafe.» Which word tells you what kind of cafe it is? | cafe | *waterside | We | sat
test: What is the waterside? | A small wooden boat | *The land along the edge of a river, lake or sea | A type of tall tree | Water in the middle of a lake
test: Which of these is a waterside plant? | A rock on the mountain top | A cup of tea | A bird that sings at night | *Reeds growing in wet soil by a river
exam: Which sentence uses waterside as a noun? | The waterside cafe was full of people. | Waterside plants grew tall along the bank. | We took a waterside walk after lunch. | *Rat sat on the waterside, watching the boats.
